[[13194139533501 50 #inst "2026-10-11T03:41:46.016Z" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186172295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168603 13194139533500 17592186045585 false] [17592186172295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168603 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186172295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405052 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186172295 1069 "q-123439" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186172295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186172295 1071 "Which of the following expressions is obtained by using the pointwise polar arc-length integrand for ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\pi /2<\\theta<\\pi /2$? Interpret the endpoint integrals as improper integrals." 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186172295 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}ds$, where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r = \\sec^{3} θ$ for $- π/2 \\le θ \\le π/2$?" 13194139533500 17592186045585 false] [17592186172295 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}ds$, where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r = \\sec^{3} θ$ for $- π/2 \\le θ \\le π/2$?" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186172295 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2$ ?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186172295 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2$ ?" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186172295 1072 #uuid "f58568c3-ce47-5162-ad81-da8c754e43d8" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186172295 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({sec}^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta +9{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186172295 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({sec}^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta +9{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186172295 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({sec}^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta +9{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese endpoint integrals are improper and diverge to $+\\infty.$ The requested expression is determined by the pointwise arc-length integrand on $-\\pi/2<\\theta<\\pi/2.$" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186198625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194351 13194139533500 17592186045585 false] [17592186198625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194351 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405086 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186198625 1069 "q-161862" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186198625 1071 "![](images/e6/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F = ⟨y, 0, 2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)A A A A A A AdA$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533500 17592186045585 false] [17592186198625 1071 "![](images/e6/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F = ⟨y, 0, 2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)A A A A A A AdA$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198625 1071 "![](images/e6/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186198625 1071 "![](images/e6/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186198625 1071 "![](images/e6/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186198625 1072 #uuid "74d7a79e-d57c-5768-84fc-b3ae4dae67d9" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198625 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨y,0,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cone ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194357 13194139533500 17592186045585 false] [17592186198626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194357 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405092 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1069 "q-161863" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1071 "Consider the vector field $F = ⟨1, z, 0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinders $z = 4x^{2}$ and $z = 16 - 4y^{2}$. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)A A A A A A AdA$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533500 17592186045585 false] [17592186198626 1071 "Consider the vector field $F = ⟨1, z, 0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinders $z = 4x^{2}$ and $z = 16 - 4y^{2}$. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)A A A A A A AdA$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,z,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinders $z=4{x}^{2}$ and $z=16-4{y}^{2}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186198626 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,z,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinders $z=4{x}^{2}$ and $z=16-4{y}^{2}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186198626 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,z,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinders $z=4{x}^{2}$ and $z=16-4{y}^{2}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1072 #uuid "87ce70cc-a7a3-5bf0-ac23-45c7dbb8a56d" 13194139533374 17592186045585 true] [17592186198626 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=16-4{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=4{x}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cylinder ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(16-4{y}^{2})=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(16-4{y}^{2})=-8y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=z=16-4{y}^{2},\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 0-(16-4{y}^{2})\\cdot (-8y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(128y-32{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cylinder ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2})=8x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=z=4{x}^{2},\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 8x-4{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-8x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(128y-32{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}8x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}128y-32{y}^{3}+8x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405039 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405038 1069 "q-115261" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405038 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405038 1071 "Given that the tangent line approximation to a differentiable function $f(x)$ at every point in an open interval containing $x=\\sqrt{2}$ strictly overestimates the values of the function at every other point in that interval, which of the following must be true?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186405038 1071 "Given that the tangent line approximation to the function $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2},$ which of the following must be true?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186405038 1071 "Given that the tangent line approximation to the function $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2},$ which of the following must be true?" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405038 1072 #uuid "1aea0f50-2894-540a-8561-3e930ff1c7d7" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405038 1074 "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function, we have\n\n$$\nL(x)>f(x),\n$$\n\nthat is, the line $L(x)$ must be above the graph of $f(x)$ near the point  $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186405038 1074 "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function, we have\n\n$$\nL(x)>f(x),\n$$\n\nthat is, the line $L(x)$ must be above the graph of $f(x)$ near the point  $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405038 1074 "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at every point $a$ in the given neighborhood strictly overestimates the values of the function at every other point, we have\n\n$$\nL_a(x)>f(x)\\quad (x\\ne a),\n$$\n\nthat is, each tangent line $L_a(x)$ lies above the graph of $f(x)$ throughout the neighborhood, with equality only at $x=a.$ A differentiable function whose graph lies below each of its tangent lines on an interval is concave down on that interval.\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186405045 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405046 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405045 1069 "q-123316" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405045 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405045 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nIII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5}$ for $n\\ge 1$" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186405045 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nIII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5}$ for $n\\ge 1$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405045 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 2$\nII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nIII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5}$ for $n\\ge 1$" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186405045 1072 #uuid "fd4fed36-e750-5401-88f4-2ac9e7e598e7" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405045 1074 "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence I,  the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence II, the limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{n}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence III, the dominant term in the denominator is ${n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{n}-3)}{(1+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-3}{1+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence I diverges." 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405059 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405058 1069 "q-123458" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405058 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405058 1071 "Which of the following gives ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s$ with the arc-length integrand simplified pointwise in the given polar angle $\\theta$ (on each smooth piece, without reparametrizing), where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi $ ?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186405058 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi $ ?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186405058 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi $ ?" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405058 1072 #uuid "9debd71f-7b2d-5a32-ae10-ca946736e9d6" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405058 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta })^{2}+{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )+\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1-{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )[1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186405058 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta })^{2}+{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )+\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1-{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )[1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405058 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhere $1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta>0$, $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nApplying the arc-length formula piecewise between the finitely many zeros of $r$, the expression for our line integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta })^{2}+{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )+\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1-{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )[1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186405065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405066 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405065 1069 "q-126539" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405065 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405065 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green's theorem and changing to polar coordinates $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with the integrand simplified pointwise before integration?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186405065 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186405065 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405065 1072 #uuid "a9b65d2f-1d10-5cc6-91ba-5ed12d5b8509" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405065 1074 "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c2/c2718471780b5ea4f4b0298624d14a22efb317ef4e8302b9269eeeec9449ca0d.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405073 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405072 1069 "q-126564" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405072 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5},\\,x+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green's theorem and changing to polar coordinates $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with the integrand simplified pointwise before integration?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186405072 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5},\\,x+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186405072 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5},\\,x+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405072 1072 #uuid "88dc642a-08f7-5754-86d1-6d7942af6e8c" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405072 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405080 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405079 1069 "q-126748" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405079 1071 "![](images/65/6571fcdf94818485c2a39e90e018d46e9c95e1acbe0a8cf2f67c688d160a347c.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186405079 1071 "![](images/65/6571fcdf94818485c2a39e90e018d46e9c95e1acbe0a8cf2f67c688d160a347c.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405079 1071 "![](images/65/6571fcdf94818485c2a39e90e018d46e9c95e1acbe0a8cf2f67c688d160a347c.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186405079 1072 #uuid "d0f4b84a-3d7c-5e64-bd13-2b1a225fb6c3" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405079 1074 "Our simple, piecewise smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186405079 1074 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186405079 1074 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405099 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405098 1069 "q-161896" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405098 1071 "![](images/14/1422e6534c001214335dcddb8888f7e2e9344e440379c47da38514e9a3ef0bd8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,2⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 true] [17592186405098 1071 "![](images/14/1422e6534c001214335dcddb8888f7e2e9344e440379c47da38514e9a3ef0bd8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,2⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186405098 1071 "![](images/14/1422e6534c001214335dcddb8888f7e2e9344e440379c47da38514e9a3ef0bd8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,2⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405098 1072 #uuid "0d7ee053-a1f8-527b-9332-a3761ff9b930" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405098 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z=1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q=0,\\,R=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x+2\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x+2\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405106 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405105 1069 "q-161897" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405105 1071 "![](images/6a/6af3d84594e2b2648dcbbae05a64f03ef5a38c76f5a1ed835fc4c6b6f50ef97a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-x,\\,-z⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined