 denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨{z}^{2},0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186405120 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405119 1069 "q-161903" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405119 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-z,\\,2y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwi