5585 true] [17592186405196 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=\\pi $, in ascending powers of $(x-\\pi )$ up to and including the term in $(x-\\pi )^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(\\pi )=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=-1.\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405196 1072 #uuid "f2480529-9e51-5e8c-9f4c-f59546697173" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405196 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=\\pi $:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\pi ),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi ).$\n\nWe substitute $y(\\pi )=1$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(\\pi ) & ={y}^{2}(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=2y{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi )$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi ) & =2y(\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+{y}^{2}(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =2(1)(-1)(0)+(1)^{2}(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯ \\\\  & =1+(x-\\pi )\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2}\\cdot (0)+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{6}\\cdot (-1)+… \\\\  & =1-(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533500 17592186045585 true] [17592186405197 1075 #edb/ref 17592186405198 13194139533501 17592186405019 false] [17592186405197 1